From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 13:07:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): ctrl+d and enter answer an exited process
 (KD-24)

Reported by the user, from use. A pane whose child has exited prints

  [process exited with code 56 (0x00000038)]
  You can now close this terminal with Ctrl+D, or press Enter to restart.

and on a ghostty pane neither key does anything. The message comes from the
connection, so it appears on both engines and promises the same two things on
both; only cascadia keeps the promise.

Both keys are the host's, not the terminal's. Cascadia answers them in
ControlCore::SendCharEvent: with the connection at >= Closed, 0x04 raises
CloseTerminalRequested and CR raises RestartTerminalRequested.

A ghostty pane never reaches that path, which is why copying the branch into
GhosttyControlCore::SendCharEvent alone would have looked right and changed
nothing. On cascadia, Terminal::SendKeyEvent returns no output for a key down
the layout maps to a character, so the key is reported unhandled and Windows
raises CharacterReceived. ghostty_surface_key encodes Enter and ctrl+d itself
and returns true, and TermControl suppresses the character event for a handled
key - so SendCharEvent is never called for either key.

So the answer goes on the key path, ahead of the surface. GhosttyExitKeyFor is
a pure function - unit tested with no surface, no engine and no keyboard, like
the other two rules in that file - and decides which promise a press is:

  enter, shift+enter        restart
  ctrl+d, ctrl+shift+d      close
  ctrl+enter                nothing; it is LF, and cascadia does not restart
  alt+enter, alt+d          nothing; alt+enter is WT's fullscreen binding
  anything else             goes to the surface, as before

Keyed on the virtual key rather than on the produced character because
GhosttyKeyTextFor drops every control character on purpose, so ctrl+d and Enter
both come back empty and there is nothing to compare. Two knowingly-small
differences from cascadia's character rule follow: ctrl+m restarts a cascadia
pane and not a ghostty one, and a layout that moves `d` closes on the physical
key. Both are recorded in the code.

The release is swallowed with the press, so a surface that has just been told
to go away is not handed a lone key-up. The check sits ahead of the read-only
guard deliberately: a read-only pane whose process is gone still has to be
closable, which is cascadia's behaviour too. SendCharEvent gets the same two
answers matched on the character, for the IME commit or injected VK_PACKET that
would arrive without a key event we recognised.

What restart still does not do: TerminalPage::_restartPaneConnection calls
HardResetWithoutErase() first, and GhosttyControlCore's is a no-op because
libghostty has no non-erasing hard reset - its `reset` binding action erases,
which is the one thing that name forbids. A shell that exited normally leaves
no dirty modes, so the case the message is about is fine; a client killed
mid-alt-screen is not. That needs a new C entry point, so it is escalated
rather than decided here, and KD-24 records it.

unitControl 70 -> 71.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 54 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp  | 41 ++++++++++++++
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h    | 19 +++++++
 .../UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp     | 38 +++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 152 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 381aa0894..961d24422 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -919,6 +919,40 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
                                              const ControlKeyStates modifiers,
                                              const bool keyDown)
     {
+        // Once the child has exited, ctrl+d and Enter are the host's keys, not
+        // the terminal's - the connection has just printed a message promising
+        // that they close the pane and restart it. Cascadia keeps that promise
+        // in SendCharEvent, which a ghostty pane never reaches: libghostty
+        // encodes Enter and ctrl+d itself and reports the key handled, so no
+        // CharacterReceived event follows. Answered here instead, ahead of the
+        // surface, and the release is swallowed with the press so the dead
+        // surface is not handed a lone key-up. See KD-24.
+        //
+        // Before the read-only check on purpose: a read-only pane whose process
+        // is gone still has to be closable, which is cascadia's behaviour too.
+        if (_connection && _connection.State() >= TerminalConnection::ConnectionState::Closed)
+        {
+            switch (GhosttyExitKeyFor(vkey, modifiers.Value()))
+            {
+            case GhosttyExitKey::Close:
+                // Already on the UI thread - this is TermControl's key handler.
+                if (keyDown)
+                {
+                    CloseTerminalRequested.raise(*this, nullptr);
+                }
+                return true;
+            case GhosttyExitKey::Restart:
+                if (keyDown)
+                {
+                    // The host answers by handing us a fresh connection.
+                    RestartTerminalRequested.raise(*this, nullptr);
+                }
+                return true;
+            default:
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+
         if (!_surface || _readOnly)
         {
             return false;
@@ -1020,6 +1054,26 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     // connection rather than pasting it. See GD-15.
     bool GhosttyControlCore::SendCharEvent(const wchar_t ch, const WORD /*scanCode*/, const ControlKeyStates /*modifiers*/)
     {
+        // The same two post-exit keys as TrySendKeyEvent, matched on the
+        // character exactly as cascadia matches them. In practice the key path
+        // above answers first and nothing arrives here; this is for the char
+        // that reaches us without a key event we recognised - an IME commit, an
+        // injected VK_PACKET - so the promise does not depend on which path the
+        // character took. See KD-24.
+        if (_connection && _connection.State() >= TerminalConnection::ConnectionState::Closed)
+        {
+            if (ch == L'\x04')
+            {
+                CloseTerminalRequested.raise(*this, nullptr);
+                return true;
+            }
+            if (ch == L'\r')
+            {
+                RestartTerminalRequested.raise(*this, nullptr);
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
+
         if (!_surface || _readOnly)
         {
             return false;
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
index 6a3da9a30..22e5c0721 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
@@ -84,4 +84,45 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         return GhosttyKeyText(std::wstring_view{ buf, gsl::narrow_cast<size_t>(n) });
     }
+
+    // Deliberately keyed on the virtual key rather than on the text the layout
+    // produces, which is what cascadia matches: its check lives in
+    // SendCharEvent and compares the character against 0x04 and CR.
+    //
+    // A ghostty pane cannot ask the same question that way. The text path here
+    // is GhosttyKeyTextFor, and it drops every control character on purpose -
+    // ctrl+d and Enter both come back empty - so there is nothing to compare.
+    // The vkey is what is left, and it is the more direct question anyway.
+    //
+    // Two knowingly-small differences from the character rule, both harmless:
+    // ctrl+m produces CR and restarts a cascadia pane but not a ghostty one,
+    // and a layout that puts `d` somewhere other than VK_D closes on the
+    // physical D key rather than on the letter.
+    GhosttyExitKey GhosttyExitKeyFor(const uint16_t vkey, const uint32_t modifiers) noexcept
+    {
+        const auto ctrl = (modifiers & (LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED | RIGHT_CTRL_PRESSED)) != 0;
+        const auto alt = (modifiers & (LEFT_ALT_PRESSED | RIGHT_ALT_PRESSED)) != 0;
+
+        // Alt is never part of either promise, and alt+enter is WT's own
+        // fullscreen binding - it must not reach this at all.
+        if (alt)
+        {
+            return GhosttyExitKey::None;
+        }
+
+        // ctrl+enter is LF rather than CR, and cascadia does not restart on it.
+        if (vkey == VK_RETURN && !ctrl)
+        {
+            return GhosttyExitKey::Restart;
+        }
+
+        // Shift is allowed: ctrl+shift+d is still 0x04, so cascadia closes on
+        // it too.
+        if (vkey == 'D' && ctrl)
+        {
+            return GhosttyExitKey::Close;
+        }
+
+        return GhosttyExitKey::None;
+    }
 }
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
index e9b328d6b..5ec507ddd 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
@@ -54,4 +54,23 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     // Pass 0 for the modifiers to get the unshifted text, which is what key
     // bindings match against.
     std::string GhosttyKeyTextFor(uint32_t modifiers, uint16_t vkey, uint16_t scanCode);
+
+    // What a key press means once the child is gone.
+    enum class GhosttyExitKey
+    {
+        None,
+        Close,
+        Restart,
+    };
+
+    // The two keys the connection promises the user after the child exits:
+    //
+    //     [process exited with code 56 (0x00000038)]
+    //     You can now close this terminal with Ctrl+D, or press Enter to restart.
+    //
+    // They belong to the host, not to the terminal, and a pane whose connection
+    // is Closed has to answer them itself. See KD-24.
+    //
+    // Modifiers are a ControlKeyStates bitfield, as everywhere else here.
+    GhosttyExitKey GhosttyExitKeyFor(uint16_t vkey, uint32_t modifiers) noexcept;
 }
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
index 3559969e4..9138e31bf 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(PrintableTextPassesThrough);
         TEST_METHOD(ControlCharactersAreNotText);
         TEST_METHOD(ShiftComesFromTheEventNotTheThread);
+        TEST_METHOD(TheKeysThatAnswerAnExitedProcess);
     };
 
     // The scan code passes through untouched. Spelled out with the values that
@@ -127,4 +128,41 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         // encoding. Proves the ctrl bit reaches ToUnicode at all.
         VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyKeyTextFor(LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED, 'C', 0x2E).empty());
     }
+
+    // The pane's process has exited and the connection has printed "You can now
+    // close this terminal with Ctrl+D, or press Enter to restart". Which key
+    // means which, and - as much as it matters - which keys mean neither.
+    //
+    // These are the only keys a closed connection answers itself; everything
+    // else still goes to the surface. KD-24.
+    void GhosttyKeyTests::TheKeysThatAnswerAnExitedProcess()
+    {
+        // The promise, both halves.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::Restart == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_RETURN, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::Close == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::Close == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', RIGHT_CTRL_PRESSED));
+
+        // Shift rides along with both: ctrl+shift+d is still 0x04, and
+        // shift+enter is still CR, which is what cascadia matches on.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::Restart == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_RETURN, SHIFT_PRESSED));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::Close == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED | SHIFT_PRESSED));
+
+        // ctrl+enter is LF, not CR. Cascadia does not restart on it, so neither
+        // do we - it goes to the surface like any other key.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_RETURN, LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED));
+
+        // alt+enter is WT's fullscreen binding and alt+d is a readline word
+        // kill. Neither is part of the promise.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_RETURN, LEFT_ALT_PRESSED));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', LEFT_ALT_PRESSED));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED | LEFT_ALT_PRESSED));
+
+        // A bare `d` types a letter, even here.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor('D', 0));
+
+        // And the neighbours of both keys, so a widened rule fails here.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor('C', LEFT_CTRL_PRESSED));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_ESCAPE, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyExitKey::None == GhosttyExitKeyFor(VK_SPACE, 0));
+    }
 }
